# Untitled

## Mesoporous metal–organic framework materials
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a new type of porous materials for diverse applications. Most open MOFs reported to date are microporous (pore sizes \(<2\ \mathrm{nm}\)), and only a small fraction of MOFs with ordered mesoscale domains \((2{-}50\ \mathrm{nm})\) is reported. This tutorial review covers recent advances in the field of mesoporous MOFs (mesoMOFs), including their design and synthesis, porosity activation and surface modification, and potential applications in storage and separation, catalysis, drug delivery and imaging. Their specificities are dependent on the pore shape, size, and chemical environments of the cages or channels. The relationship between the structures and functions is discussed. The future outlook for the field is discussed in the context of current challenges in applications of mesoporous materials.
## 1. Introduction
Porous materials are found in all classes of materials, from microporous zeolite to mesoporous silica and metal oxides and to macroporous polymers.1 They are fundamental in a diverse range of applications from structural materials to energy technologies, which have wide applications in catalysis, adsorption, ion-exchange and host–guest assembly. The classification of porous materials is usually given by the diameter of their pores. According to IUPAC, microporous \((<2\ \mathrm{nm})\), mesoporous \((2{-}50\ \mathrm{nm})\) and macroporous \((>50\ \mathrm{nm})\) materials are distinguished.2 Note that this definition is based on a measurement of porosity, not only a crystal structure that seems porous. As shown in Fig. 1, there are two types of representative
adsorption isotherms for mesoporous solids (Types IV and V).1,2 Both of them are characteristic of a hysteresis loop owing to capillary condensation occurring in mesopores and are observed in practice, but by far the most common is Type IV.
Traditionally, mesoporous materials have been either organic or inorganic materials. Inorganic mesoporous solids such as silicas and metal oxides are usually crystalline (having ordered structures) but fully inorganic and thus suffer from structural diversity, functional tailorability and mechanical strength, whereas organic mesoporous solids such as activated carbon are amorphous, or to be strict, partially ordered in polycrystalline form of graphite or diamond within an amorphous carbon matrix from a crystallographic sense.1 In contrast, ordered mesoporous organic–inorganic hybrid materials such as periodic mesoporous organosilicas may not only combine the respective beneficial characters of organic and inorganic components but also often exhibit exceptional properties that exceed what would be excepted for a simple mixture of the components.1
