Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers3 have recently emerged as a class of very promising organic–inorganic hybrid materials for diverse applications.4–13 MOFs are crystalline porous materials built from metal ions/polynuclear building units connected by polytopic organic linkers. An important feature of MOFs is that their framework structures, pore environment and functionality can, at least in principle, be finely controlled by the choice of metal and organic building blocks and how they are connected. While high micropore volumes and large surface areas are desirable for many applications, such narrow pores do not allow for hosting large objects and anchoring molecular functions, as well as fast mass diffusion and transfer, thereby limiting their uses, for example, in storage, separation, catalysis and drug delivery.4 In contrast, mesoMOFs may meet the demands for the growing applications emerging in processes involving large molecules owing to large pore volumes and high surface areas. Additionally, the ability to fabricate homochiral mesoMOFs can expand the utility of MOFs into the area of asymmetrical catalysis, recognition and separation.14 These motivations thus spark the proliferation of mesoMOF materials.
However, most of the reported MOFs to date are restricted to microporous regime, and only a small fraction of MOFs with mesoporous structures are reported.15–29 Note that the pores of mesoMOFs refer not only to channels but also to cavities. MOFs with large cavities \((>\!2\;\mathrm{nm})\) but small connecting channels \((<\!2\ \mathrm{nm})\) such as \(\mathrm{MIL-100}(\mathrm{Cr})^{15}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})^{16}\) \(\mathbf{\Psi}(\mathbf{MIL}\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}\) Material Institute Lavoisier), are also described as mesoMOFs, even though they do not exhibit the Type IV isotherms.
This is due to the fact that the adsorption profiles of mesoMOFs mainly depend on their pore shapes, which are governed by the size of the accessible window into the pore. In principle, the use of a linear extension of the organic linker may enhance the pore size of MOFs in a given network, but sometimes the pore diameter achievable in such MOFs is limited by interpenetration, a case of mutual growth of two or more networks in a structure where the networks are physically but not chemically linked.22 Even though, this bridging-ligand-enlength approach has predictable outcomes and has greatly facilitated the intentional construction of mesoMOFs.5 Another attempt has been made by synthesis of MOF materials using surfactants as structure directing agents (SDA), the removal of which resulted in hierarchically porous MOFs with tunable mesoporosity.30–32 Nevertheless, the search of new mesoMOF materials with higher surface areas remains a major challenge in the rapidly expanding field of MOFs.
Despite being in the embryonic stage, mesoMOFs have not only achieved great success in the application domains of microporous MOFs (microMOFs) and traditional mesoporous materials but also exhibited some superiority to the micro/ mesoporous counterparts. The progress on MOFs has been widely reviewed, but an overview of the research of mesoMOFs is missing.33 We present here recent advances in the design and synthesis of mesoMOFs and illustrate the applications in guest storage and separation, heterogeneous catalysis, drug delivery and imaging. The review covers mesoMOFs with the case studies being organized according to synthetic strategy, synthesis, porosity activation, surface modification and applications.
## 2. Synthetic strategy
## 2.1. Synthetic methods
MesoMOFs, like microMOFs are generally synthesized by the solvothermal technique.4 A routine solvothermal recipe involves addition of all the reactants (metal salts, ligands and solvents) in one step in sealed vessels and then heating at elevated temperature (typically \(<\!250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) under subcritical conditions. Crystalline products could be generated within several days. Solvothermal conditions can facilitate the equilibration of the reacting system
