and the subsequent formation of ordered structures via an enhanced diffusion process and optimized coordination interactions in a thermodynamically more favourable fashion.
The microwave-assisted method is so far not often used for the synthesis of mesoMOFs, but is an invaluable technique to perform high speed synthesis. This is a good approach to control the size and morphology of particles.34 Both solvent evaporation and diffusion methods have been extensively employed to synthesize microMOFs, but, with only one exception, have not yet been used to prepare mesoMOFs so far.24
Surfactant-assisted synthesis, as an efficient soft-templating approach to mesoporous silicates, has also been developed for fabricating mesoporous particles of MOFs.30–32 In the soft-templating approach, surfactant molecules (soft templates) first self-assemble into micelles and then serve as templates for directing the formation of mesopores to afford the mesoporous silicates after removal of surfactant, while for construction of mesoporous particles of MOFs, the crystalline microporous frameworks are simultaneously generated during the formation of mesopores. The as-synthesized particles are characteristic of the hierarchical arrangement of micro/mesopores. Moreover, the size of mesopores can be facilely adjusted by introduction of different SDAs and variation of the synthetic conditions.30
## 2.2. Ligands and secondary building units
2.2.1 Ligands. The increase of the channel sizes of microMOFs to mesoporous range remains a great challenge. Ligand extension is an apparent strategy, but open MOFs built from extended ligands tend to disintegrate after the removal of guest molecules. Another difficulty in the synthesis of a mesoMOF by ligand extension is that such products are often accompanied by framework interpenetration that drastically reduces the size of the pores.8 Therefore, the construction of mesoMOFs should be guided by the extension of the ligand while inhibiting interpenetration and reinforcing the framework against collapse upon guest removal.
As the linkers of mesoMOFs, ligands should be firstly rigid enough to ensure that frameworks will be sufficiently robust to sustain permanent pores (as shown by gas sorption). So, typically the ligands consist of rigid phenyl or polyphenyl scaffolds bearing coordination sites and functional groups with limited flexibility and rotation degree (Fig. 2). Also, the coordination bonds should be sufficiently strong. Accordingly, carboxylate linkers are among the most important and widely used bridging ligands for constructing mesoMOFs owing to the high thermal and chemical stabilities of the carboxylate–metal fragment.5 Moreover, MOFs based on carboxylate linkers are often presented with neutral skeleton, which may greatly reduce the potential of collapse of the frameworks during the porosity activation process, whereas the ionic hybrid or zeolitic solids frequently encounter inaccessible pores even upon extraction of the counterions or templates. In contrast, polytopic aromatic N-containing ligands such as pyridyl- and imidazolyl derivatives are not frequent choices for mesoMOFs.21,29,35
2.2.2 Secondary building units. Secondary building units (SBUs) are elucidated as the geometry of the units defined by the points of extension in the context of MOFs.5 Based on this definition, organic ligands with well-defined points of extension
are regarded as SBUs, too. In this section, we mainly focus on the inorganic SBUs.5 Similar to organic linkers, inorganic SBUs for mesoMOFs should be sufficiently robust and are often composed of various di- and polynuclear building units built from strong metal–oxygen bonds. It is thus possible to predict the network topology by considering the geometric and chemical attributes of SBUs and organic linkers. The coordination-directed assembly of judiciously designed rigid SBUs into pre-determined ordered structures is a powerful way to synthesize mesoMOFs.5 This process is concerned with the topology of the structures and their invariance whatever the chemical associations. This means that, whatever the size of SBU, the topology of the resulting structure remains invariant and, in terms of porosity, 'the larger the SBUs, the larger the pores'. The key points to apply this approach depend on the existence of a given type of SBUs in the primitive solution and subsequent preservation during crystallization of desired MOFs. This has been proved to work in the synthesis of, for example, isoreticular (having the same framework topology) mesoporous IRMOFs- \(n\) , which are built from tetrahedral \(Z\!\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}\) polynuclear building units and linear dicarboxylates.22 Note that topology is a basic mathematics field in which any networks are reduced to a series of nodes and connections. As far as MOFs are concerned, we can reduce chemical entities such as SBUs to nodes and bridging ligands to connections. The topology of networks depends on the way in which they are connected, not on the shape or size of the individual components.
As an extension to SBUs, supranolecular building blocks (SBBs) have been recently proposed for construction of MOFs.6 SBBs can be treated as enlarged SBUs with more complexity and higher connectivity in larger length scale, which will lead to larger pore size and an improved control over the topology. For example, three 1,3-benzenedicarboxylic acid (m-BDC) moieties in tritopic hexacarboxylate ligands direct the reticulation of the paddle wheel \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{4}\) polynuclear building units into large truncated cuboctahedra and an isoreticular series of (3,24)-connected mesoMOFs.18–20
## 3. Synthesis of mesoMOF materials
According to the pore shape and structure, mesoMOFs can be classified into cage-type and channel-type.33 The structure of the first type has mesocages delimited by windows either sharing faces or separated by spacers, while the latter possesses mesopores accessible in the form of channels in at least one dimension. The chirality could be facilely implanted into mesoMOFs either by stereoselective synthesis using enantiopure species (reactants, solvents, templates) or by spontaneous resolution upon crystallization without any chiral auxiliaries.14 The preparation of chiral crystalline mesoporous frameworks would permit many new applications. In this section, several model mesoMOFs with cage-type, channel-type and chiral structures are selected to illustrate their synthesis and structural features (Table 1). In addition, the assembly of mesoporous particles from microMOFs is discussed as well.
## 3.1. Cage-type mesoMOFs
The most famous cage-type mesoMOFs may be MIL-100(Cr) and MIL-101(Cr). In 2004, Férey et al. reported the synthesis
and structure of \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Cr})\) for the first time.15 MIL\(100(\mathrm{Cr})\) is obtained in polycrystalline form by hydrothermal reaction of metallic \(\mathrm{Cr}\), HF, and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\right)\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) at \(220\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The trimeric \(\mathrm{Cr}\) polynuclear building units serve as SBUs and are connected by BTC to afford a super tetrahedron (ST) in which the trimers occupy the four vertices and four BTC linkers span the triangle faces. The resultant STs delimit two kinds of mesocages via corner sharing mode. The smaller cage composed of 20 STs has an internal diameter of \(\sim\!25\mathrm{~\AA~}\) while the larger cage containing 28 STs has an internal diameter of \(\sim\!29\mathrm{~\AA~}\). Treating STs as nodes, the underlying topology is zeotypic montmorillonite in which the small cage is simplified as a dodecahedron with 12 pentagonal faces while the large cage is reduced to a polyhedron consisting of 12 pentagonal and 4 hexagonal faces (Fig. 3). Although the pores are mesoporous, the apertures of the windows are limited to be microporous.
In continuation of searching MOFs with large pores, in 2005, Férey et al. discovered another mesoMOF MIL-101(Cr).16 MIL-101(Cr) was prepared by replacing \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) with terephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BDC}\right)\) under the similar conditions and exhibited the same topology as MIL-100(Cr). Compared with MIL-100(Cr), the two cages delimited in MIL-101(Cr) have larger pore sizes owing to the increased size of STs (Fig. 3). In this case, the four vertices of the ST are still occupied by the trimers, but the six edges are surrounded by six BDC linkers. This kind of arrangement contributes to the expansion of STs.
The use of metal–organic polyhedra as building blocks for the construction of mesoMOFs is an efficient strategy, since the inherent cavities of the fused polyhedra are maintained on tessellation in space.17–20 Such an approach is exemplified by the linking of three 1,3-benzenedicarboxylate moieties, which facilitates the generation of MOFs from symmetrical 24-connected faceted polyhedra or SBBs that are based upon linked
and surface area of the resulting framework. For example, in 2010, Hupp et al. synthesized the extra-high porosity MOF NU-100/PCN-610, \(\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(TTEI)(H_{2}O)_{3}]n}\), by the solvothermal reaction of the elongated rigid \(C_{3}\)-symmetric hexacarboxylate ligand \(\mathrm{H_{6}TTEI}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5H_{2}O}\) in \(\mathrm{DMF/HBF_{4}}\) at \(75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). The non-catenated 3D framework of NU-100 is built up of square \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}]\) paddlewheels connected through six-branched TTEI linkers, which act as hexagonal building blocks with the three isophthalate groups of each TTEI coplanar as anticipated. NU-100 has three types of cavities. The largest spheres that can fit in these cavities, based on the van der Waals surface of the framework interior, were computed to be 1.3, 1.5 and \(2.7~\mathrm{nm}\) in diameter. The largest open cage length in this MOF is \(4.8~\mathrm{nm}\). After activation with supercritical carbon dioxide (SCD), NU-100 exhibits an ultrahigh BET surface area of \(6143~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are built of imidazoles and tetrahedral metal ions with networks that resemble those of zeolites due to the similar bridging angle to the \(145^{\circ}\) Si–O–Si bond angle. The greater basicity of the imidazolate linker, as compared to the typical carboxylate linkers, results in stronger metal–ligand bonds and, therefore, ZIFs show high thermal stability and chemical robustness. In 2008, mesoMOFs ZIF-95 and ZIF-100 were prepared by heating 5-chlorobenzimidazole (cbIM) and \(\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O}\) or \(\mathrm{Zn(O_{3}SCF_{3})_{2}}\) in DMF and water at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Restricting the water content of the reaction medium used for ZIF-95 synthesis promotes the crystallization of a new phase ZIF-100. Less hydrated conditions are achieved by using anhydrous \(\mathrm{Zn(O_{3}SCF_{3})_{2}}\) in place of \(\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3}){\cdot}4H_{2}O}\). Control experiments rule out the anions as SDAs. ZIF-95 have a neutral network with all Zn nodes tetrahedrally coordinated by 5-chlorobenzimidazole.
There exist two types of ellipsoidal shape cages: from the van der Waals surfaces, the larger cage measures \(30.1 \times 20.0\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\) and the smaller cage measures \(25.1 \times 14.3\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\). In ZIF-100, of the ten independent Zn atoms that form the (3,4)-coordinated net, nine are connected to four cbIM linkers and the remaining one is only tri-coordinated by cbIM. The most notable aspect of the ZIF-100 structure is a giant cage with 264 vertices, which is one of the most complex polyhedra found in an inorganic material. This cage has a \(67.2\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) outer sphere diameter and a \(35.6\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) inner sphere diameter. After solvent exchange and activation under vacuum and heating, ZIF-95 and ZIF-100 exhibit Langmuir surface areas of 1240 and \(780\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively.
## 3.2. Channel-type mesoMOFs
In 2002, Yaghi et al. reported the first 3D mesoMOF, IRMOF-16, that was synthesized by heating \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) and \(p\)-terphenyl-\(4,4^{\prime}\)-dicarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC}\right)\) in DEF at \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 4 days. This MOF has the primitive cubic net adopted by the prototype IRMOF-1 (MOF-5) in which robust \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{6}\) polynuclear building units act as SBUs and vertices that extend into a 3D cubic porous framework. In the structure of IRMOF-16, the free- and fixed-diameter values are 19.1 and \(28.8\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\), respectively. The open mesochannels are accessible from three directions, leading to the framework highly porous with free volume up to \(91.1\%\). The calculated crystal density is as low as \(0.21\ \mathrm{g\ cm}^{-3}\), which is the lowest density reported for any crystalline material known to date. By changing the organic linker type, different mesoporous IRMOFs named as IRMOFs-8, -10, -12 and -14 were obtained (Fig. 5). Variations in the organic linkers not only change the pore size but also generate materials with different chemical functionalities and affinities for guest molecules. When the length of the spacer increased, interpenetrating isomers were produced. Therefore, this family of mesoporous IRMOFs was synthesized under more dilute conditions to avoid interpenetration. The successful synthesis of IRMOFs represents an advanced step for search of high porous MOFs with large pores and makes the microMOFs update to mesoMOFs for the first time.
In 2006 Zhou et al. reported the synthesis and structure of mesoMOF-1 with 3D channels. MesoMOF-1 was synthesized
by heating the flexible ligand \(4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}\)-s-triazine-1,3,5-triyltrip-p-aminobenzenecarboxylic acid (H3TATAB) and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in DMF at \(90\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In this structure, four TATAB are connected with six \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\) paddlewheels to form a \(T_{d}\)-octahedron, which can act as vertices to build a cuboctahedron via corner sharing. The cuboctahedra propagate to a 3D framework with open channels of \(22.5 \times 26.1\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\) from all three directions. Notably, the amino groups in TATAB not only present flexibility, but also provide a platform for modification. By treating with HX \((\mathrm{X} = \mathrm{F}, \mathrm{Cl}, \mathrm{Br})\), the ionic frameworks exhibit significantly enhanced stability \((300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) compared with the pristine network \((180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and give rise to a typical Type IV \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm. The HCl-treated mesoMOF-1 exhibits a BET surface area of \(685\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
MesoMOFs with large 1D channels can be constructed from infinite rod-shaped SBUs. In 2007, Qiu and Zhu et al. reported the first mesoMOF (JUC-48) with 1D channels, constructed from infinite rod-shaped cadmium SBUs. JUC-48 crystallized from a solution of \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\) (biphenyl-\(4,4^{\prime}\)-dicarboxylic acid) in DMF by slow diffusion with triethylamine at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) over 3 days. The Cd centers are linked together by carboxylate groups of BPDC to generate 1D metal chains, which are interconnected through BPDC to form a 3D noninterpenetrating extended network with etb topology and 1D hexagonal channels of \(24.5 \times 27.9\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\) viewed along the \(c\)-axis direction. The tubular channel has an impenetrable wall based on the rod-shaped SBUs, which prohibits additional ligands from filling in between adjacent linkers and the interpenetrated structure. JUC-48 exhibits a typical Type I \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm, with a Langmuir surface area of \(880\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
Another mesoMOF (UMCM-1) with 1D hexagonal channels that is built of different types of ligands was reported by Matzger et al. in 2008. UMCM-1 was prepared from the mixed solution of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB}\), and \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in DMA at \(85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for two days. By variation of the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}/\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB}\), the synthesized products can be gained in the form of mixtures of different MOFs. The optimal molar ratio for the pure phase product is between \(3:2\) and \(1:1\). In this structure, six BDC linkers and five BTB linkers are connected by nine \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\) SBUs to produce a microporous cage with an internal dimension of \(14 \times 17\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\). Six such cages assemble together in
an edge-sharing fashion to define the diameter of the mesopore, a 1D hexagonal channel \((2.4 \times 2.9\ \mathrm{nm}^{2})\). The micro- and mesoporous nature of UMCM-1 is confirmed by the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm, which has a typical Type IV behavior. The high \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake \((1200\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) up to the first plateau indicates a substantial microporous contribution to the sorption behavior, with a Langmuir surface area of \(4730\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The second plateau reveals a Langmuir surface area of \(6500\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (\(>1400\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption).
## 3.3. Chiral mesoMOFs
Most of the reported mesoMOFs are achiral and only a few chiral species have been reported.26–29,41 In 2008, Ma and Lin reported the first examples of homochiral mesoMOFs based on robust \(R\) -BINOL-based tetracarboxylate ligand.26 Treating copper(II) salts with robust tetracarboxylate ligand \(R\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{2\mathrm{a}}\) at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in DEF or DMF afforded two isostructural chiral mesoMOFs \(R\mathrm{-}[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2\mathrm{a}}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot12\mathrm{DEF}\cdot16\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(R\mathrm{-}[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2\mathrm{a}}\cdot\) \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot16\mathrm{DMF}\cdot19\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . In the structures, the \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}]\) paddle-wheels serve as SBUs that are (4,4)-interconnected by chiral ligands to afford enormous open channels running through all three directions of the crystals, with the largest opening of \(3.2\,\times\,1.5\,\,\mathrm{nm}^{2}\) . Interestingly, the structure constructed from racemic ligand in DEF is isostructural to the chiral counterpart while the product from DMF has two interpenetrating nets of opposite chirality. This phenomenon was ascribed to the templating effect of DEF molecules. After activation at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum, the two noninterpenetrating MOFs exhibit BET surface area of \({\sim}240\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) . The accessibility of their open channels to large reagents is shown by the Brilliant blue R-250 (BBR-250) dye uptake experiments in solution. In a subsequent report, Lin et al. showed that the above 4,4-connected network is very robust and accommodates tetracarboxylic acid ligands of varying sizes as well as both diethoxy and dihydroxy functional groups, leading to a family of isoreticular chiral mesoMOFs (CMOFs-2a–4b).27 These compounds are highly porous with a tunable void space that ranges from \(73.3\%\) to \(91.9\%\) , and thus provide an ideal platform for generating heterogeneous catalysts for asymmetric addition of alkylzinc to aldehydes after treating with \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{\prime}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}\) .
A chiral Schiff base was chosen to build salen-derived dicarboxylate ligands by Liu et al. in 2010.28 The chiral ligands \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{5}\) first reacted with \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in situ oxidation in air to yield \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{III})\) metalloligands, which further reacted with \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in DEF/EtOH or DMF/EtOH
at \(60{-}90\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to generate three mesoMOFs CMOFs-2–4 with a primitive cubic net. CMOF-3 is a two-fold interpenetrated isomer of CMOF-4. This series of CMOFs hold tunable open channels by alteration of the length of linkers, with the largest channel sizes of \(2.5\times2.3\,\mathrm{nm}^{2}\) . Again, DEF favored the formation of noninterpenetrating structures (CMOF-2 and CMOF-4) as a result of the templating effects. The accessibility of their open channels to large reagents is also evidenced by the BBR-250 dye uptake experiments in solution.
MesoMOFs are generally fabricated by one-pot procedure. In 2011, Cui et al. reported the bottom-up assembly of homochiral mesoMOFs from a pyridyl-functionalized helicate.29 The \(P\) -configured triple-stranded helicate \([\mathrm{Cu}_{7}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{L}_{6}]\) is constructed from three chiral \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) ligands and a ducubane \(\mathrm{Cu}_{7}\) unit. Heating the helicate and \(\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in DMSO/2-butanol/ \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) at 80 and \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) yielded \([\{\mathrm{Cu}_{7}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{L}_{6}\}\{\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3}.\) - \((\mathrm{S}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{10})_{2}\}\}\cdot10\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (JT-1) and \([\{\mathrm{Cu}_{7}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{L}_{6}\}_{2}\{\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{6}.\) - \((\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7})\}\cdot\{\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\}\}\cdot18\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (JT-2), respectively. The stepwise assembly process was controlled by the reaction temperature. Single phase product was obtained at 80 or \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , while a mixture of them was afforded at the intermediate temperature of \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 6). In JT-1, the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{7}\) helicate binds to six \([\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\upmu{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{S}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{10})_{2}]\) \((\mathrm{Cu}_{6}{\cdot}\alpha)\) clusters through pyridyl groups. Six \(\mathrm{Cu}_{7}\) helicates and five \(\mathrm{Cu}_{6}{\cdot}\alpha\) clusters that are related by \(C_{3}\) symmetry merge to generate a tetrahedral cage, which has an open spherical cavity with an internal diameter of \(2.36~\mathrm{nm}\) . In JT-2, the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{7}\) helicate alternatively binds to three \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]\) and three \([(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})(\upmu{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3})_{2}\) \((\upmu_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7})]\) \((\mathrm{Cu}_{6}{\cdot}\upbeta)\) clusters. The structure consists of two types of tetrahedral cages, namely a larger cage, encapsulated by six helicates, three \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]\) and two \(\mathrm{Cu}_{6}{\cdot}\upbeta\) clusters, as well as a smaller cage enclosed by four helicates, one \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]\) and three \(\mathrm{Cu}_{6}{\cdot}\upbeta\) clusters. Each type of cage has an irregular cavity that has a maximum inner width of \(\sim\!2.3\) and \(1.8\;\mathrm{nm}\) . The two compounds exhibit Type IV sorption behaviors, with BET surface areas of 375 and \(421\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. The BBR-250 dye uptake experiments proved the accessibility of their open channels to large reagents in solution as well.
## 3.4. Mesoporous particles of MOFs
The surfactant-templated approach has recently been adopted to synthesize mesoporous MOF particles, in which the walls of the mesopores are constructed by a microporous framework (Fig. 7).30–32 In 2008, Qiu et al. chose HKUST-1 as a model complex and performed solvothermal reactions in the presence
of structure-directing agent (SDA) cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) micelles under similar reaction conditions to HKUST-1.30 The mesoporous particles in which mesopore walls are constructed from the microporous framework of HKUST-1 were produced after removal of templates. The mesopore-to-micropore surface-area ratio \((S_{\mathrm{meso}}/S_{\mathrm{micro}})\), mesopore-to-micropore pore-volume ratio \((V_{\mathrm{meso}}/V_{\mathrm{micro}})\) and pore size can be tuned by varying the CTAB/Cu2+ molar ratio while keeping the framework invariable. However, the pore sizes of the above mesoporous MOF particles were limited to \(5.6\,\textrm{n m}\). Thus, 1,3,5-trimethylbenzene (TMB) was chosen as auxiliary SDA to swell the CTAB micelles and expand the mesopores. By varying the ratio of TMB/CTAB, the pore diameter can be adjusted from 14.9 to \(31.0\,\mathrm{\nm}\), as well as \(S_{\mathrm{meso}}/S_{\mathrm{micro}}\) and \(V_{\mathrm{meso}}/V_{\mathrm{micro}}\). The products exhibit BET surface areas in the range of 533 to \(1225\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherms of a mode intermediate between Type I and Type IV with hysteresis loops. TEM and selected-area electron diffraction further confirm that the mesostructured MOFs consist of nanocrystalline domains of microporous HKUST-1. This work represents the first example of the synthesis of hierarchically porous MOFs with tunable porosity.
In 2008, MacLachlan et al. adopted the same approach to fabricate mesostructured Prussian blue (PB) analogues.31 However, direct condensation of \(\mathrm{CTA}_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) with \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}\) in water or formamide only gave microcrystalline PB. So another way by tethering the SDA to the PB precursor was explored to make the desired products. As a result, the mesoporous PB analogs were obtained by reaction of \(\mathrm{Na}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{5}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and alkyl pyrazinium bromide in formamide at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to give an amphiphilic intermediate followed by treatment with \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\). PXRD and TEM show that the materials exhibit long-range periodic order on the nanometre scale, and can exhibit lamellar, hexagonal, or cubic phases. This method can be applied to a wide range of metal salts to afford a series of PB analogues with different structures. However, the SDA can't be removed while keeping the mesochannels intact owing to the strong coordination interaction between the SDA and framework, thus limiting the application range of this approach.
Ionic liquid (IL) and supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) have also been utilized as solvents together with surfactants to make mesoporous MOF particles.32 In 2011, Zhao et al. performed the reaction of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) in IL 1,1,3,3-tetramethylguanidinium acetate (TMGA) at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with 1,1,3,3-tetramethylguanidinium acetate (N-EtFOSA) as a SDA whilst \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was charged into the reaction cell to \(16.8\;\mathrm{MPa}\).31 The authors proposed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) existed as a core during the formation of cylindrical micelles while IL could facilitate the fabrication of microporous framework.
SEM, TEM and the small-angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) technique show that the materials exhibit long-range periodic order on the nanometre scale. The particles with ordered mesopores and microporous walls were formed after removal of IL, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and surfactants. The pore size was about \(3.0\ \mathrm{nm}\) and the particle size was in the 70 to \(110\,\mathrm{\nm}\) range. The product exhibits a BET surface area of \(756\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherms of typical Type IV with hysteresis loops, whereas the micropore surface area is found to be \(482\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) \((S_{\mathrm{meso}}/S_{\mathrm{micro}}=0.57)\). The above results confirm the formation of hierarchically meso/microMOF, in which the walls of the mesopores are constructed from a microporous framework.
## 4. Porosity activation and surface modification
Maximization of surface areas and functionalization of internal surfaces are key to optimize mesoMOFs for many potential applications.4,10,12 One important issue is the activation of MOFs after synthesis.7 Solvents used during synthesis usually remain in the pores of the materials and usually activation by heating is required to remove these solvent molecules. Studies have shown that activation at elevated temperatures can cause sample decomposition, whereas activation at lower temperatures greatly minimizes the danger of reducing metal ions.8 To address this issue, several novel approaches such as supercritical fluid drying36 and freezing drying37 methods are developed. On the other hand, despite the great success in obtaining functionally diverse MOFs via direct one-pot synthesis, the scope of functional groups within the MOF pores has remained relatively limited by this approach. This might be due to the fact that not all functional groups are compatible with or stable to the solvothermal conditions for MOF synthesis, which generally require high temperatures and pressures. An alternative approach for the fabrication of functionalized MOFs is by postsynthetic modification (PSM) of organic linkers and coordinated unsaturated metal ions or by adsorption of functional objects (this will be discussed in Section 5.3).9,38
## 4.1. Porosity activation
Two treatments widely adopted to remove solvents contained in mesoMOFs are (1) thermally assisted evacuation of the initially encapsulated guests (conventional activation) and (2) liquid exchange of the encapsulated solvents such as DMF and DMA for lower boiling point solvents such as MeOH and THF followed by a degas process or pore evacuation at moderate temperature.8,22 Most of the reported mesoMOFs were treated in this way to achieve reasonable surface areas (Table 1). But, in some cases, the above strategies fail to yield the expected surface areas.
In 2009, a new route for effectively enhancing the surface areas of MOF materials was reported by Hupp et al.36 They showed that the supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) process exhibited a wide range of applications for porosity activation of \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\)-based MOFs.7 Compared with the activation via solvent exchange, the surface areas of the activated MOFs were improved several-fold without loss of crystallinity and porosity. Subsequently, this supercritical drying method was used to activate a (3,24)-connected mesoMOF, NU-100 (also termed as PCN-610).20 In 2010, Zhou et al. reported that the framework of PCN-610 completely collapsed upon activation by solvent exchange,19 while the SCD approach was demonstrated to be able to remove guest molecules without damaging NU-100. The activated NU-100 had a very high BET surface area up to \(6143\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). In 2010, Yaighi et al. made use of SCD to activate four mesoMOFs (MOF-180, MOF-200, MOF-205 and MOF-210) and yield the highest surface area \((6240\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for MOF-210) close to the theoretical value.39 As suggested by Farha and Hupp,8 the high efficiency of SCD activation stems from the elimination of solvent \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\) surface tension responsible for prevention of pore collapse and easy evacuation at temperatures and pressures above the critical point. Moreover, SCD activation may prevent undesirable agglomeration of particles, a process accountable for the block of pores of MOFs.
The key nature of the SCD method lies in the elimination of surface tension during activation process. Along this line, in 2009, Lin et al. applied the freeze drying method, a relatively old technique to obtain a high surface area for a (4,4)-connected mesoMOF \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{7}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\right]\)-14DMF: \(5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\).37 This method involves exchanging the high boiling-point solvent incorporated in the framework with benzene and then removing the benzene by freeze drying. The detrimental effect of surface tension can be avoided via direct sublimation of frozen solvent under vacuum at a temperature below its freezing point, thereby enhancing the permanent porosity of a MOF. The mesoMOF showed \(29\%\) increase of Langmuir surface area after the freeze drying process. However, the PXRD pattern of activated mesoMOF illustrated that a new phase was yielded after activation due to the breathing effect.
## 4.2. Surface modification
PSM is a useful tool for introducing new functionalities without disrupting the overall structure and porosity of preexisting frameworks.9 It is obvious that only those MOFs which possess sufficient thermal and chemical stability and permanent porosity in harsh environments could be functionalized via PSM. The modified materials are very useful as both adsorbents and heterogeneous catalysts.
MIL-101(Cr) is a good candidate for PSM owing to the high stability and the potential coordinatively unsaturated metal sites (CUSs) upon removal of aqua ligands under thermal treatment. In 2008, Hwang et al. firstly achieved PSM on CUSs by amine grafting with ethylenediamine (ED), diethylenetriamine (DETA) and 3-aminopropyltrialkoxysilane (APS).40 The successful grafting was confirmed by the complete disappearance of CUSs in IR spectrum and decrease of BET surface areas and pore sizes. Moreover, the uncoordinated amino groups of grafted amines could further act as interaction sites to introduce metal nanoparticles (NPs), thereby modifying the surface once again.
In a continued work, the concept of amine grafting onto CUSs was extended to sulfonic acid grafting by replacement of ethylenediamine with cysteamine \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{SH}\right)\), followed by oxidation with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) at \(45\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).34
In 2009, Kim et al. demonstrated for the first time that chiral mesoMOFs could be fabricated from achiral MIL-101(Cr) by PSM with chiral catalytic units.41 The chiral MOFs CMIL-1 and CMIL-2 were synthesized by treating MIL-101(Cr) with L-proline derivatives [(S)-N-(pyridin-3-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide] and [(S)-N-(pyridin-4-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide] in a \(1:3\) molar ratio in refluxing chloroform for one day, respectively. The internal surface was modified via the coordination of L-proline derivatives to the coordinatively unsaturated chromium centers and the chirality was thus introduced into the framework. The products were characterized by a variety of techniques including powder X-ray diffraction and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption measurements. In contrast to unmodified parent MOFs, these two chiral MOFs are capable of catalyzing asymmetric aldol reactions with high enantioselectivity, though they have similar framework structures with MIL-101(Cr) (see Section 5.3).
The amino functionalized mesoMOFs provide great opportunities to covalently attach functional species by PSM. This has been elegantly demonstrated by Cohen and Lin's groups. In 2009, Tanabe and Cohen reported the fabrication of UMCM-1-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), which was further subjected to tandem PSM by reaction of anhydrides followed by metalation with acetylacetonate complexes to afford two heterogeneous catalysts42 while Lin et al. demonstrated that the presence of amine groups in mesoMOFs could be utilized for loading an optical imaging contrast agent and an anticancer drug through PSM for drug delivery and imaging (see Section 5.4 for details).43
Light driven processes may be an efficient and gentle method to initiate chemical reactions within a MOF lattice. In 2010, Cohen et al. reported that photolabile protecting groups can be liberated within UMCM-1 analogs to yield materials with free hydroxy groups.44 The mesoMOFs UMCM-1-\(\mathrm{OBnNO}_{2}\) and UMCM-1-\(\left(\mathrm{OBnNO}_{2}\right)_{2}\) were prepared from \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) and 2-((2-nitrobenzyl)oxy)terephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\mathrm{BnO}-\mathrm{BDC}\right)\) and 2,3-bis((2-nitrobenzyl)oxy)terephthalic acid \(\left(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\mathrm{BnO}\right)_{2}-\mathrm{BDC}\right)\), respectively, in DMF at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Scheme 1). Single-crystal X-ray diffraction of UMCM-1-\(\mathrm{OBnNO}_{2}\) and UMCM-1-\(\left(\mathrm{OBnNO}_{2}\right)_{2}\) unambiguously showed that both MOFs had the same topology as UMCM-1. Upon irradiation with \(360\;\mathrm{nm}\) light for \(24-48\;\mathrm{h}\), both UMCM-1-\(\mathrm{OBnNO}_{2}\) and UMCM-1-\(\left(\mathrm{OBnNO}_{2}\right)_{2}\) underwent a distinct color change from colorless to orange and were converted into UMCM-1-OH and UMCM-1-CAT via the photochemical removal of 2-nitrobenzyl groups, as evidenced by NMR and single-crystal X-ray diffraction. Moreover, UMCM-1-OH and UMCM-1-CAT exhibited significant increases in porosity, which was consistent with the liberation of the bulky nitrobenzyl substituents. The exposed hydroxyl groups of UMCM-1-CAT were proven to be accessible by the change in absorbance spectrum upon metalation with \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\), indicative of the formation of UMCM-1-FeCAT.
## 5. Emerging applications
MesoMOFs will surely expand the using scope of MOFs due to combination of common merits of microMOFs with large
pore sizes and volumes that can, for example, enhance mass transfer and host large molecules.\(^1\) Owing to their extremely plentiful structures and compositions, mesoMOFs have been developed as multifunctional materials to display versatile excellent properties, such as gas/liquid storage and separation, catalysis, hosting guest molecules or nanoparticles, drug delivery and imaging.\(^{4,12,33}\)
## 5.1. Gas storage and separation
Hydrogen is an environmentally friendly energy carrier and clean fuel. A major challenge in the practical use of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is to develop an efficient storage system. The US Department of Energy (DOE) set the \(5.5~\mathrm{wt\%}\) and \(40\mathrm{~g~L^{-1}}\) 2015 hydrogen storage targets.\(^{45}\) Carbon dioxide and methane are two of the principal greenhouse gases emitted today. The DOE has also set targets for carbon dioxide \((56\,\mathrm{wt\%})\) and methane \((18\,\mathrm{wt\%})\) storage systems.\(^{46,47}\) MicroMOFs have demonstrated great potential as host matrix for storage and separation of the strategic and greenhouse gases \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) etc.\(^{11,12}\) In particular, mesoMOFs can facilitate the uptake of gases at high pressure, and, in some cases, the hierarchical arrangement of micro/mesopores will undoubtedly optimize the adsorption capacity.\(^{17-20}\) Recent theoretical and experimental studies revealed the correlation between the surface area or the pore volume and the amount of gas adsorbed, as well as the increase of the adsorption enthalpy for MOFs with open metal sites, active organic functionalities and/or metal nanoparticles.\(^{48}\)
MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Cr})\) and MIL-101(Cr) showed high uptake capacities for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\).\(^{49,50}\) In 2006, Fe´rey et al. showed that MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Cr})\) and MIL-101b(Cr) could adsorb 3.3 and \(6.1\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) hydrogen at \(8\ \mathrm{MPa}\) and \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\), respectively.\(^{49}\) The higher storage capacity for MIL-101b(Cr) was ascribed to the higher adsorption enthalpy \((\sim10\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}\) at low pressure) caused by the presence of strong adsorption sites. In 2008, the same group reported \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) storage capacities of MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Cr})\) and MIL-101c(Cr).\(^{50}\) The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake of MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Cr})\) was \(18\ \mathrm{mmol\ g^{-1}}\) at \(5\ \mathrm{MPa}\) and \(303\,\mathrm{~K~}\), while
the adsorbed amount for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) was \(119\,\mathrm{cm}_{\mathrm{STP}}^{3}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) at \(3.5\,\mathrm{MPa}\) and \(303\ \mathrm{K}\). MIL-101c(Cr) has a similar adsorption capacity for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) to MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Cr})\), whereas exhibits a record \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) storage capacity of \(40\ \mathrm{mmol\ g^{-1}}\) or \(390\ \mathrm{cm}_{\mathrm{STP}}^{3}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) \((303\ \mathrm{K},\ 5\ \mathrm{MPa})\). This leads to enormous volumes of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) per volume of adsorbent, with a value of \(390\ \mathrm{v/v}\). The initial adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Cr})\) and MIL-101(Cr) corresponds to adsorption at CUSs via strong coordination followed by filling of the residual porosity. Note that saturation occurs with mesoMOFs at much higher pressures than zeolites, which may be interesting for the recovery of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at high pressures from gas streams. Moreover, both MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Cr})\) and MIL-101(Cr) could be regenerated for recycled use for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) storage for ten times over a three-month period.
The spillover process, involving dissociation of hydrogen adsorbed on one surface (metal) and transfer of monoatomic hydrogen onto another surface, has recently been adopted to fabricate \(\mathrm{Pt/Pd}\)-doped MOFs for enhanced hydrogen storage applications.\(^{51}\) In 2010, Latroche et al. showed that \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Al})\) with \(\sim2\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{Pd}\ \mathrm{NPs}\) exhibited significant changes in hydrogen sorption behaviors with respective to the pristine MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Al})\).\(^{52}\) The location of \(\mathrm{Pd}\ \mathrm{NPs}\) in the mesopores reduced the BET surface area and led to obvious decrease of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at \(77\ \mathrm{K}\) compared with MIL- \(100(\mathrm{Al})\). However, the excess \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorption of \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Al})\) \((0.35\ \mathrm{wt\%})\) is doubled to the pristine material \((0.19\ \mathrm{wt\%})\) at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) and \(4\ \mathrm{MPa}\). This may be attributed to the steep increase in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at low pressure caused by the formation of \(\beta\)-palladium hydride and consequent spillover effect from \(\mathrm{Pd}\) particles to the framework.
In the (3,24)-connected mesoMOFs, the open polyhedral SBBs provide micropores suitable for gas storage whilst the mesocages offer enormous open space for pore volumes.\(^{17-20}\) This type of MOFs exhibited very high uptake capacities for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Among them, the benchmark NU-100 showed the highest excess \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake of \(99.5\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}}\) at \(77\ \mathrm{K}\) and 56 bar as well as total \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake of \(2315\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}}\) at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) and 40 bar.\(^{20}\) The significant role for CUSs in low-pressure
adsorption was verified by the neutron powder diffraction (NPD) for NOTT-112.\(^{18}\)
ZIFs have a high affinity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) due to a large amount of nitrogen atoms embedded in the framework.\(^{7}\) ZIF-95 and ZIF-100, combined huge cavities and highly constricted windows, showed strong affinity to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at low pressure (800 Torr) and \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) with uptake \(\sim\!1\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\).\(^{21}\) On the contrary, the uptake of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) was nearly negligible for them due to the aperture sizes (3.65 Å and \(3.35\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\)), respectively) comparable to the kinetic diameter of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (3.3 Å). The disproportionately high affinity and capacity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) implied potential utility in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture and storage. Breakthrough experiments indicated that ZIF-95 and ZIF-100 could selectively adsorb \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from mixtures of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (50 : 50 v/v) (Fig. 8). The average selectivities of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), CO and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) were \(4.3:1\), \(11.4:1\) and \(18.0:1\), respectively, for ZIF-95 and were \(5.9:1\), \(17.3:1\) and \(25:1\), respectively, for ZIF-100. In all cases, ZIFs exhibited much higher selectivity compared with a bituminous coal-based activated carbon (BPL).
In 2010, the highest amounts of excess \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and total \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake by MOFs were reported by Yaghi et al.\(^{39}\) Among the four mesoMOFs (MOF-180, MOF-200, MOF-205 and MOF-210), MOF-210 showed unprecedented uptake amounts of \(2400\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (298 K and 60 bar) and \(176\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (77 K and 70 bar) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), respectively (Fig. 9). The excess \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) uptake was also among the highest values, up to \(264\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) and 80 bar for MOF-210. The high adsorption capacity for this series of mesoMOFs was attributed to the ultrahigh surface areas (up to \(6240\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\)), void volume (up to \(90\%\)), and pore volume (up to \(3.59\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) as well as low crystal density (as low as \(0.22\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\)).
## 5.2. Liquid phase adsorption and separation
Adsorption from liquid solution is more complex with respect to gas phase adsorption because both solute and solvent compete for the solid surface.\(^{53}\) The adsorption of a solute is dependent on its molecular size and chemical properties, on the textural properties of the adsorbent and on its pore surface chemistry. Owing to the high porosity and large surface area, as well as readily accessible large cavities or channels in solution, mesoMOFs can adsorb and separate not only small substances such as aromatic hydrocarbons, but also large objects such as polycyclic organic dyes, nanoscale metal clusters and metal particles (this will be discussed in Section 5.3).
MesoMOFs show very high uptakes of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) such as benzene. In 2009, nanoscale particles of MIL-101(Cr) synthesized by microwave irradiation were employed for the removal of benzene from aqueous solution and compared to activated carbon.\(^{34}\) The sorption capacities on MIL-101(Cr) and active carbon were examined using aqueous solutions containing 1000 ppm benzene at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\). In spite of their similar pore volumes and hydrophobic nature, MIL-101(Cr) adsorbed much higher amount of benzene (10.8 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) than activated carbon (6.5 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) and exhibited higher uptake rate, which was most likely due to the larger pore diameter and/or smaller particle size. A similar result was obtained for adsorption of benzene in vapor-phase.
In addition to the fast sorption behavior, MIL-101(Cr) also adsorbed hydrocarbons such as n-hexane readily at lower vapor pressures, a very useful property for the sorptive removal of VOCs that are generally present at low concentrations. In contrast, the sorption capacities of hydrocarbons on mesoporous silicates are very low at low relative pressures. This is a typical example where MOFs outperform a material that is used in industry for this application.
MesoMOFs can function as containers for nanosized substrates such as polyoxometalates and organic dyes. In 2005, F´erey et al. reported the size selectivity of MIL-101(Cr) in the adsorption of Keggin polyanions by aqueous adsorption of the Keggin salt.\(^{16}\) The van der Waals radius of the anion of \(\mathrm{K}_{7}\mathrm{PW}_{11}\mathrm{O}_{40}\cdot n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) is \(\sim\!13.1\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) and fits well with the aperture size of large cage (\(\sim\!14.5\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times16.0\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\)) in MIL-101(Cr), which rules out the diffusion of Keggin anions into the small cages (\(\sim\!12.0\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times12.0\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\)). Quantitative analysis revealed large adsorption amount with an estimation of \(\sim\!0.05\) Keggin anions per chromium.
In 2007, Qiu and Zhu et al. showed that mesoMOF crystals of JUC-48 with Rhodamine 6G (Rh6G) dye molecules were obtained by adding Rh6G in ethanol to the growth solution, or by immersing the crystals in the ethanol solution of the dye.\(^{24}\) The amount of Rh6G in the red-colored doped crystals was about \(0.046\%\). Upon excitation at \(541\ \mathrm{nm}\), the JUC-48 adduct displayed temperature-dependent emission spectra characteristic of encapsulated Rh6G. The intensity of emission peak at \(563\ \mathrm{nm}\) increased linearly with the decrease of temperature (298–77 K), implying the potential application in temperature-sensing devices. Frustrated by the discrepancy between the expected and experimental surface areas, in 2010, Lin et al. developed a straightforward dye-uptake assay to access the porosity of mesoMOFs.\(^{27,28}\) Brilliant blue R-250 (BBR-250) was chosen as the probe molecule because of the proper dimension of \(2.2\times1.8\ \mathrm{nm}^{2}\). Two series of isoreticular chiral mesoMOFs (CMOFs-2a–4b and CMOFs-2–4) were examined
in this way to prove the capability to transport large reagent and product molecules during the catalysis process. Although the initial aim is to test the accessibility of open channels in mesoMOFs, as a bonus, the BBR-250 uptake was really significant, up to \(124~\mathrm{wt\%}\). In 2011, Cui et al. employed this dye-uptake approach demonstrating the mesoporous nature of chiral helicate-based mesoMOFs in solution (BBR-250 uptake \(\sim\!21.4\%\)).29
Chromatography (including gas- and liquid-phase) is one of the most powerful separative methods in analytical chemistry. Porous silica gel and alumina are usually used as solid adsorbents.1 MesoMOFs have been applied as stationary phases for chromatography. In 2010, Gu and Yan made a MOF-coated capillary column for high-resolution gas chromatography (GC) separation for the first time by using MIL-101(Cr) as the stationary phase.54 The \(15\,\mathrm{~m~}\) long column was prepared by loading a fused silica capillary column ( \(0.53\mathrm{~mm}\) inner diameter) with a suspension of MIL-101(Cr) or pyridine grafted MIL-101(Cr) in ethanol \((2~\mathrm{mg~mL}^{-1})\). The column had an approximately \(0.4\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) thick MIL-101(Cr) coating on the inner wall. The mixture of \(p\) -xylene, \(o\) -xylene, \(m\) -xylene and ethylbenzene can be baseline separated by the as-prepared column within \(1.6\ \mathrm{min}\) without the need for temperature programming, due to the difference in the polarity of the isomers. The capability of that column was further demonstrated by high-resolution GC separation of two other isomer mixtures of substituted benzene derivatives, as well as a mixture of n-alkanes with a broad range of boiling points. The high efficiency is believed to originate from the host–guest interactions, CUSs and suitable polarity of MIL-101(Cr).
In 2010, Xu et al. prepared three non-, micro- and mesoporous Cd-MOF isomers with pillar-layered structures from \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -bipyridine and dicarboxylate linkers.35 The Cd mesoMOF
was utilized as stationary phase for liquid chromatography (LC) separations. The open channel of Cd mesoMOF has a dimension of \(1.7\,\times\,2\;\mathrm{nm}^{2}\), which facilitated the incorporation of Rh6G \((1.3\,\times\,1.6\ \mathrm{nm}^{2})\) whereas exhibited size-exclusion of the larger dye Brilliant Blue R-250 \((1.8\,\times\,2.2\ \mathrm{nm}^{2})\) (Fig. 10). Whereas the Cd microMOF isomer \((0.8\times1.5\,\mathrm{nm}^{2})\) with smaller pores could not work. This is the first report of a mesoMOF as an LC stationary phase for large molecule separation.
## 5.3. Heterogeneous catalysis
The use of mesoMOFs as solid catalysts is significantly interesting because their large pore sizes are favourable for the diffusion of large substrates and products.54 Furthermore, it is often possible to tailor the pore structure and functionality to yield the chemo-, regio-, stereo- and/or enantioselectivity by creating an appropriate environment around the catalytic center in the restricted space available. Moreover, it is possible to have a homogeneous distribution of one or more active sites due to their high crystalline degree.55 A variety of structural features can engender catalytic activity in mesoMOFs, such as open metal sites at framework nodes, which often have unique coordination environments, and metal or organic centered catalytic units that are integrated via PSM, as well as active metal nanoparticles that are grown and stabilized within the cavities.9,38 However, MOFs still have issues concerning thermal stability and chemical robustness, due to the nature of the components of the materials. MesoMOF catalysis is still at its infancy and there is much new territory to be explored. In this section, the reported catalysts are divided into three categories: frameworks for catalysis, postsynthetic modification for catalysis and encapsulation of metal nanoparticles for catalysis, according to the origin of catalytic sites in the porous network.
5.3.1 Frameworks for catalysis. In 2008, Kaskel et al. demonstrated the high catalytic activity of MIL-101(Cr) towards cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde with trimethylsilylcyanide at \(313\,\mathrm{~K~}\).56 Compared with \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\), MIL-101(Cr) exhibited much higher yield of \(98.5\%\) after \(3\,\mathrm{~h~}\) even if a lower catalyst loading of only \(0.5\,\mathrm{mol\%}\) is used. Moreover, the active catalytic sites of chromium(II) can resist the reduction of benzaldehyde, which is vital for recycle use. A recycle test with three consecutive runs showed that MIL-101(Cr) could be reused for several times with a somewhat reduced activity.
In 2010, both PCN-100 and PCN-101 were reported to display size- and shape-selectivity for Knoevenagel reaction. The weakly basic amine groups on the linkers make PCN-100 and PCN-101 quite suitable for Knoevenagel reaction (Fig. 11).57 The highest conversion (93% and 96%) was observed with benzaldehyde of smallest size \((0.61\,\times\,0.87\,\,\mathrm{nm}^{2}\); shape: linear) and a decreased yield (58% and 65%) was achieved for the larger 4-phenylbenzaldehyde \((0.61\,\times\,1.33\,\,\mathrm{nm}^{2}\); shape: linear) while the conversion for benzophenone \((0.66\,\times\,1.14\,\,\mathrm{nm}^{2})\), a linear molecule with potential product extending in three directions, was
negligible. The dramatic difference in conversion rates demonstrated the size- and shape-selectivity of two mesoMOFs.
In 2010, Lin et al. reported that asymmetric epoxidation of olefins with high activity was catalyzed by chiral mesoporous CMOF-2 and CMOF-4 assembled from metallosalen ligands.28 The \(\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}\) ions and 2-(tert-butylsulfonyl)iodosylbenzene acted as active catalytic sites and oxidant, respectively. The conversion rates for epoxidation were directly proportional to channel sizes and the CMOF-2 and CMOF-4 gave conversion rates even comparable to that of a homogeneous catalyst owing to the larger open sizes. In contrast, the interpenetrated CMOF-1, CMOF-3 and CMOF-5 exhibited much slower epoxidation reaction rate. To evaluate the influence of open channel sizes on conversion and confirm that catalysis dominantly occurred interiorly, large crystals of CMOF-4 \((\sim\!0.4\,\times\,0.4\,\times\,0.4\,\,\mathrm{nm}^{3})\) and CMOF-1 \((\sim\!0.3\,\times\,0.3\,\times\,0.3\,\,\mathrm{nm}^{3})\) were used for catalysis without stirring to reduce the impact of surface catalytic sites. 35% conversion and 84% enantiomeric excess (ee) were observed for CMOF-4 with larger open channel size while 10% conversion and 78% ee were detected for CMOF-1 with
smaller channel. These results proved the open size-dependent conversion and the more accessible catalytic sites within mesoMOFs for substrates and oxidant. In addition, CMOF catalysts showed good recyclability and reusability.
5.3.2 Postsynthetic modification for catalysis. In 2008, F´erey et al. employed amine grafted MIL-101 (Cr) for base-catalyzed Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde and ethyl cyanoacetate.40 Comparisons were made between mesoporous silica SBA-15 and pristine MIL-101(Cr) as well as ED-SBA-15 and ED-MIL-101(Cr). In both cases, MIL-101(Cr) behaved better than SBA-15. In particular, the highest yield of \(97.1\%\) and selectivity of \(99.1\%\) were achieved by the ED-MIL-101(Cr) with TOF up to \(328\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\), which was 10 times higher than APS-SBA-15. Remarkably, ED-MIL-101(Cr) revealed size dependence on catalytic activities when the benzaldehyde was replaced by larger benzophenone.
In 2009, Tanabe and Cohen reported that tandem PSM could be used to introduce Lewis acid sites into \(\mathrm{UMCM-1-NH}_{2}\) for catalyzing Mukaiyama-aldol reactions of aldehydes and silyl enols.42 UMCM-1-AMFesal and UMCM-1-AMCupz were generated by firstly reacting \(\mathrm{UMCM-1-NH}_{2}\) with 3-hydroxyphthalic anhydride and 2,3-pyrazinedicarboxylic anhydride followed by metalation with \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\), respectively. UMCM-1-AMFesal showed moderate conversion \((\sim\!58\%)\) in \(24~\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature for two aldehyde substrates over three catalytic cycles. Control reactions showed the catalytic activity originated from the introduced Lewis acid centers. Size selectivity was observed with UMCM-1-AMFesal when a larger silyl enol was used, as evidenced by no turnover even after \(24~\mathrm{h}\).
In 2009, Kim et al. reported that L-proline-grafted MIL-101(Cr) (CMIL-1 and CMIL-2) could efficiently catalyze the aldol reaction between various aromatic aldehydes and ketones in excellent yields \((60{-}90\%)\) and with good enantioselectivities (ee \(55–80\%\) ).41 The catalytic activity is comparable to corresponding homogenous catalysts while enantioselectivity is much higher than the homogenous analogue. The better enantioselection was ascribed to the confinement effect together with multiple chiral inductions in the mesocages. Notably, CMIL-1 exhibited size-selectivity associated with the aperture size of mesocages and thus proving that the catalysis occurred mostly in the cavities of the catalysts. Moreover, the solid catalysts can be recycled at least up to three times without obvious loss in reactivity and enantioselectivity.
In 2010, Lin et al. reported that a series of isoreticular homochiral MOFs (CMOFs-1b–4b) functioned as tunable platforms for asymmetric diethylzinc or alkynylzinc addition to aromatic aldehydes via PSM on the dihydroxy groups in the BINOL scaffold with \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}\) (Fig. 12).27 Ti-modified mesoporous CMOF-3b was chosen to screen the catalytic reaction and showed almost complete conversion (up to \(>99\%\) ) and high selectivity (up to \(99\%\) ) as well as high ee (up to \(91\%\) ) comparable to the homogenous catalyst. Moreover, CMOF-3b can be recycled up to five times without deterioration of conversion, selectivity and ee. Interestingly, the ee values were dependent on the channel sizes, increasing with the increase of pore sizes. Thus, CMOFs-3b and -4b with larger channel sizes exhibiting higher ee may be due to the enhancing diffusion of
aromatic aldehydes and diethylzinc reagents and products. Addition of alkynylzinc to aromatic aldehydes was also carried out to demonstrate the generality of Ti-modified CMOFs. Similar results were observed with somewhat reduced ee due to the larger sizes of substrates and products, which stress the importance of large pores or channels for catalysis.
5.3.3 Encapsulation of metal nanoparticles for catalysis. MesoMOFs can be used as host matrices to support the catalysts, as in the case of mesoporous silicates.38 Most efforts have focused on encapsulation of metal nanoparticles inside the cavities of mesoMOFs (metals@mesoMOFs), which can be synthesized in solution- or gas-phase loading methods, depending on the different metal precursors. In particular, the incipient wetness impregnation is a mostly used method for this fabrication, because common inorganic salts are adoptable as precursors. The mechanically robust walls of the frameworks can be utilized both to confine metal nanoparticles and to restrict their growth. The good dispersivity of NPs within the frameworks leads them more accessible to reactants, thereby exhibiting excellent catalytic activity. Moreover, combination of active metal NPs with the functionalities within the host, multifunctional catalysts capable of promoting different reactions or one-pot cascade reactions can thus be realized. A preferred system for catalyst study is \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{mesoMOFs}\),40,56,58 and other systems including \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{mesoMOFs}^{59}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{mesoMOFs}^{60}\) are also attractive. The catalytic reactions are mostly focused on oxidation of alcohols, hydrogenation reaction, and C–C coupling reaction (Table 2).
The \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{mesoMOFs}\) were firstly tested for C–C coupling reaction. Early in 2008, Kuwang and F´erey et al. demonstrated the \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ED}\)-grafted-MIL-101(Cr) and \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{APS}\)-grafted-MIL-101(Cr), which were obtained by anionic exchange reactions followed by \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\) reduction, had obviously high activities during the Heck reaction of acrylic acid with iodobenzene, and their activities were comparable with that of a commercial \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst \((1.09\mathrm{\wt\%}\mathrm{\Pd})\) after a certain induction period \((0.5–1\mathrm{~h})\).40 The good recyclability of Pd-loaded MIL-101(Cr) verified the heterogeneous catalysis nature.
In 2010, Li and He et al. reported the one-pot cascade synthesis of methyl isobutyl ketone (MIBK) from acetone catalyzed by \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\), which was obtained by a simple impregnation method.58 MIBK is manufactured via a three-step
process involving condensation, dehydration and hydrogenation. The first two steps are acid-catalyzed and can be fulfilled by the \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) centers in \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\), while the last step can be promoted by embedded Pd NPs (Fig. 13). High Pd loading \((0.87\%\) and \(2.79\%\) ) catalysts significantly reduced the selectivity of MIBK, while low Pd loading \((0.05\%)\) decreased the conversion of MIBK (Fig. 14). The \(0.11{-}0.34\,\mathrm{~wt}\%\) Pd loading was found to be suitable for the desired selectivity and conversion, probably due to the monodispersion of NPs throughout the pores. The best performance of \(0.11\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\) obtained at \(150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) afforded \(90.2\%\) MIBK selectivity at \(60\%\) conversion. The \(0.11\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) Pd loading catalyst can be easily recycled up to five times without loss of catalytic efficiency and crystallinity of the framework.
## 5.4. Drug delivery and imaging
The storage capacity and release of drug in a porous container are governed by a variety of factors including pore size, shape, connectivity and host affinity.10 In traditional porous materials such as silica and polymeric matrixes, drug loading capacity is usually not sufficiently high and encapsulated drug is difficult to be released specifically. To achieve a high loading and a
controlled release, porous materials possessing large volumes and regular structures are desired. MesoMOFs with tunable pore size and functionality thus provide a wealth of opportunities for drug delivery.10,43,61,62 However, this area has not been explored as broadly as gas storage and gas separation applications. Another interesting application of mesoMOFs includes materials incorporating organic functional moieties and biocompatible paramagnetic or superparamagnetic metal ions in the structures. The metal centers may function as magnetic resonance (MR) contrast agents in either \(T_{1}\) - or \(T_{2}\) -weighted imaging and the organic moieties could serve as optical contrast agents.
In 2006, \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Cr})\) and \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\) were firstly tested for their abilities for delivery of Ibuprofen by F´erey et al.61 They showed remarkable Ibuprofen adsorption with \(0.347\mathrm{~g~g~}^{-1}\) and \(1.376\;\mathrm{~g~}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. The dramatic difference in Ibuprofen adsorption can be explained by the selective occupation of the larger cages in \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Cr})\), whereas all the cages are filled in \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\). The complete release of Ibuprofen was achieved for \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Cr})\) and \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\) after 3 and 6 days, respectively. In comparison with MCM-41 (\(\sim\!3.6\ \mathrm{nm}\) cage size), \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\) exhibited four times larger Ibuprofen uptake and much longer releasing time (2 days for MCM-41),
probably due to the stronger interaction between Ibuprofen and the pore surface of MIL-101(Cr) (\(\pi-\pi\) and acid–base interactions). However, chromium is well known for its toxicity (not biocompatible), thereby limiting further clinical application.
To reconcile the cytotoxicity and high drug loading capacity, in 2010, the non-toxic nanoscale Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-101\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) along with several microporous iron(III) carboxylate MOFs were fabricated by F´erey et al. for drug delivery and imaging.62 For biological applications, the nanoscale MOFs particles were coated with several relevant polymers (such as PEG) to prevent aggregation. A major degradation occurred after seven days of incubation at \(37\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for Fe-MIL-100, indicating a reasonable in vitro degradability. Acute in vitro toxicity experiments were carried out after intravenous administration of Fe-MIL-100 with dose up to highest amount \((220\,\mathrm{~mg~}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) in Wistar female rats. The low toxicity was verified by the reversible weight increase and return to normality after injection in one to three months as well as the absence of immune or inflammatory reactions. The amphiphilic antitumoural drug busulfan (Bu) was loaded into Fe-MIL-100 by simply soaking in saturated drug solutions. Fe-MIL-100 showed exceptionally high drug loading \((25\,\mathrm{wt\%})\) and avoided the use of toxic organic solvents in chemotherapy and reduced the consequent liver toxicity with smaller dosing amount. The same activity was obtained for entrapped Bu as free Bu owing
to entrapped Bu in its molecular form within the pores. In a similar way, Fe-MIL-100 was used for drug carrier for azidothymidine triphosphate (AZT-TP), cidofovir (CDV) and doxorubicin (doxo). High drug loading was also achieved with molecular sponge Fe-MIL-100. A progressive release of AZT-TP, CDV and doxo was observed using Fe-MIL-100 while 'burst' release was observed with MIL-53 bearing pore size smaller than AZT-TP. Subsequent in vitro anti-HIV test of entrapped AZT-TP showed a significant anti-HIV activity with (AZT-TP) charged Fe-MIL-100 at a concentration of \(200\,\mathrm{\nM}\) in AZT or AZT-TP. In all cases, Fe-MIL-100 outperformed other microporous iron(III) carboxylate MOFs both in drug loading capacity and control of drug release.
The paramagnetic nature of \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\) in this MIL family along with the metal-coordinated water and/or free water molecules within the framework provide them good candidates for MR contrast agents. High transversal \((r2)\) relaxivity values of 95 and \(92\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\,\mathrm{mM}^{-1}\) were obtained at \(9.4\,\mathrm{T}\) for PEG-coated MIL-88A and Fe-MIL-100. MR measurements were performed in Wistar female rats and the treated organs became darker than the normal ones, indicating the application of MR contrast agents.
In 2009, Lin et al. presented an alternative way to use nanoscale Fe-MIL-101\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) for drug delivery and imaging via PSM (Fig. 14).43 The presence of amino groups on the
particles allowed for covalent attachment of biologically relevant cargoes by PSM. An optical imaging contrast agent was firstly loaded by treating the NPs with BODIY-Br (1,3,5,7-tetramethyl-4,4-difluoro-8-bromomethyl-4-bora-3a,4a-diazas-indacence) in THF at rt. The BODIPY loading was determined to be \(5.6{-}11.6\ \mathrm{wt\%}\). Confocal microscope images of the BODIPY-loaded particles with HT-29 cells demonstrated fluorescence, which was an indication for the penetration of the particles through cell membrane and release of the fluorescent cargoes. The ESCP (ethoxysuccinato-cisplatin), a prodrug of cisplatin, was also loaded by treating the NPs with ESCP, which was first activated by 1,1-carbonyldiimidazole in DMF at rt. Thus, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-functionalized MIL-101(Fe) NPs provided an efficient platform for delivering the ESCP prodrug with an overall payload of \(12.8\ \mathrm{wt\%}\). However, these NPs were not stable in PBS buffer at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and experienced rapid degradation. To improve the control of degradation, these particles were coated with a thin silica layer, resulting in a longer half-life of 16 and \(14\ \mathrm{h}\) for the BODY- and ESCP-loaded particles, respectively, in PBS buffer at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) compared to 2.5 and \(1.2\ \mathrm{h}\) of the uncoated particles. Functionalization of silica-coated particles with cyclic peptide c(RGDfk) showed that these NPs had cytotoxicity comparable to that of cisplatin when treated against HT-29 cells. The work affords a valid approach for the design of a wide range of nanomaterials for imaging and therapeutic applications.
## 6. Conclusions
With tremendous progress achieved in synthesis of mesoMOFs with new structural topologies, enormous opportunities remain for engineering such hybrid frameworks as multifunctional materials. In this review, we have summarized recent advancements in design and synthesis, activation and modification of mesoMOF materials and their applications in storage and separation, catalysis, drug delivery and imaging.
The SBUs or SBBs expansion approach has greatly facilitated the intentional construction of MOFs with defaulted structures, implying rational design to some extent, and may pave the way to mesoMOFs. Solvothermal reactions have proven to be an effective approach for the synthesis of mesoMOFs with high crystallinity. Microwave-assisted synthesis facilitates generation of mesoMOFs with uniform sizes and good crystallinity, whereas surfactant-assisted synthesis presents a promising way to assemble mesoMOF particles. The developments in activation and modification of mesoMOFs demonstrate how modern physical techniques and chemical design can be applied to maximize their surface areas and functionalize their internal surfaces. The supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) process has emerged as a quite efficient way to activate mesoMOFs to achieve ideal specific areas that cannot be realized by traditional thermal-assisted evacuation or solvent exchange, while PSM provides an opportunity not only to impart functional sites but also to reveal underlying functionalities.
MesoMOFs facilitate the uptake of gases at high pressure, while the hierarchical arrangement of micro/mesopores optimizes the adsorption and separation capacity. MesoMOFs have the ability to selectively adsorb large amounts of substrate molecules such as organic dyes and drugs from the liquid phase.
The adsorption capacity strongly depends on surface chemistry, pore size and shape, as well as pore volume. The use of mesoMOFs as LC and GC stationary phases for substrate separations has also appeared. Functionalized mesoMOFs have demonstrated high efficiency and/or selectivity in some catalytic processes and shown excellent recyclability and reusability. Such performances are comparable to homogeneous counterparts because of the rapid mass transfer rates of reactants and products in mesostructures. Encapsulation of nanoparticles inside mesoMOF cavities has greatly extended their applications in catalysis and hydrogen storage by catalytic spillover. One prominent merit for mesoMOFs lies in the possibility to exert homochirality for enantioselective processes. Additionally, mesoMOFs provide a platform for controlled drug delivery and imaging owing to well defined pore shapes and sizes as well as limited aperture sizes.
Overall, the development in mesoMOF materials represents a key step towards finding new hybrid multifunctional materials and has significantly expanded the applicable ranges of MOFs and traditional mesoporous materials. Although mesoMOFs have been shown to outperform other porous materials in some applications, it is worth noting that issues such as cost and stability of materials have not yet been taken into account. Formidable issues to be solved for both exploratory and user-oriented research are the low cost synthesis of more functional mesoMOFs with thermal stability and chemical robustness. With continuous research efforts towards these challenges, we have every reason to believe that mesoMOF materials will play important roles for the current societal problems of energy, sustainable development and health.
